# Android Telephony Rules

## Runtime Permissions

- Every call into `TelephonyManager` (or any API that reads cellular/radio
  state) MUST be preceded by a runtime check that both of the following
  permissions are granted:
  - `ACCESS_FINE_LOCATION`
  - `READ_PHONE_STATE`
- On Android 10 (API 29) and above, cell info and signal strength APIs
  require `ACCESS_FINE_LOCATION` in addition to `READ_PHONE_STATE`. Do not
  assume `READ_PHONE_STATE` alone is sufficient.
- Never guard a `TelephonyManager` call with only a manifest declaration.
  Manifest entries are necessary but not sufficient — always verify via
  `ContextCompat.checkSelfPermission(...) == PackageManager.PERMISSION_GRANTED`
  (or the project's permission-checking utility) immediately before the call.
- If permissions are missing, fail gracefully (return empty/default state,
  log, or trigger a permission request) rather than letting the call throw
  a `SecurityException`.

## TelephonyCallback, not PhoneStateListener

- All code that observes cellular data (signal strength, service state,
  data connection state, cell info, etc.) MUST use `TelephonyCallback` via
  `TelephonyManager.registerTelephonyCallback(...)`.
- `PhoneStateListener` is deprecated (API 31+) and MUST NOT be used in new
  or modified code. Prefer `TelephonyCallback.CellInfoListener` (and related
  non-privileged listeners). Do **not** use
  `TelephonyCallback.PhysicalChannelConfigListener` in this app —
  it requires `READ_PRECISE_PHONE_STATE` or carrier privileges and throws
  `SecurityException` (`enforceCarrierPrivilege`) for a normal third-party app.
- Always unregister callbacks (`unregisterTelephonyCallback`) when the
  owning component is destroyed/stopped to avoid leaks.

## CellInfo bandwidth and Carrier Aggregation

- Bandwidth MUST come from `CellIdentityLte.getBandwidth()` (kHz) for LTE.
  Public `CellIdentityNr` has no bandwidth API on compileSdk 35 — report
  [CellInfo.UNAVAILABLE] for NR until a privileged/alternative source exists.
  Do not use `PhysicalChannelConfig` for bandwidth.
- Carrier aggregation MUST be detected from `CellInfo.getCellConnectionStatus()`:
  count cells with `CONNECTION_PRIMARY_SERVING` or
  `CONNECTION_SECONDARY_SERVING`; CA is active when that count is greater
  than one. Secondary band labels come from cells with
  `CONNECTION_SECONDARY_SERVING` only.
- Prefer an immediate `TelephonyManager.requestCellInfoUpdate(...)` after
  registering `CellInfoListener` to seed the first snapshot.
- Map raw `CellInfo` into the domain model `CellSnapshot` before exposing
  data outside the data layer. Do not let framework telephony types leak
  into `domain/` or `ui/` layers.

## Package Conventions

- `data/telephony` — TelephonyManager/TelephonyCallback wrappers, permission
  checks, and mapping from Android framework types (e.g. `CellInfo`) into
  domain models.
- `domain/model` — plain Kotlin domain models such as `CellSnapshot`,
  decoupled from Android telephony APIs.
- `ui/screens` — Compose screens/state that consume domain models only,
  never framework telephony types directly.
